</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2018</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Masked generative models have become a strong paradigm for text-to-motion synthesis, but they still treat motion frames too uniformly during masking, attention, and decoding.
This is a poor match for motion, where local dynamic complexity varies sharply over time.
We show that current masked motion generators degrade disproportionately on dynamically complex motions, and that frame-wise generation error is strongly correlated with motion dynamics.
Motivated by this mismatch, we introduce the Motion Spectral Descriptor (MSD), a simple and parameter-free measure of local dynamic complexity computed from the short-time spectrum of motion velocity.
Unlike learned difficulty predictors, MSD is deterministic, interpretable, and derived directly from the motion signal itself. We use MSD to make masked motion generation complexity-aware.
In particular, MSD guides content-focused masking during training, provides a spectral similarity prior for self-attention, and can additionally modulate token-level sampling during iterative decoding.
Built on top of masked motion generators, our method, DynMask, improves motion generation most clearly on dynamically complex motions while also yielding stronger overall FID on HumanML3D and KIT-ML.
These results suggest that respecting local motion complexity is a useful design principle for masked motion generation. Project page: <a href="https://xiangyue-zhang.github.io/DynMask" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://xiangyue-zhang.github.io/DynMask</a></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Motion Generation, 3D Human Motion Generation, Mask Motion Modeling
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29655v1/figures/f_teaser.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/148;" width="215" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Motivation.<span id="S1.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<em id="S1.F1.7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left</em>: standard masked motion generation allocates uniform modeling budget across all frames, applying the same masking, attention, and sampling regardless of local motion difficulty—this leads to degraded quality on dynamically complex motion.
<em id="S1.F1.7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right</em>: DynMask uses the Motion Spectral Descriptor (MSD), a DCT-based per-frame complexity signal, to make masked generation complexity-aware: more supervision, stronger attention exchange, and broader decoding exploration are directed toward dynamically difficult frames, yielding better motion quality.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text-to-motion generation aims to synthesize realistic 3D human motion from natural language descriptions, with applications in animation, gaming, virtual reality, and embodied AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tevet et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinyoanuntapong et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinyoanuntapong et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Among the major paradigms—diffusion models, autoregressive token models, and masked generative transformers—masked motion generation has emerged as an especially attractive choice.
It supports parallel decoding, is naturally compatible with editing and inpainting, and achieves strong benchmark performance by reconstructing motion tokens from partial context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
However, a fundamental limitation remains: current masked motion generators treat frames too uniformly during mask allocation, attention, and iterative decoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This uniform treatment is a poor match for motion, whose local difficulty varies sharply over time.
Frames in static poses or smooth transitions are easy to infer from context, whereas frames at kicks, turns, jumps, or rapid phase transitions are much harder.
Yet standard masked motion generation applies nearly the same reconstruction policy to all of them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, causing errors to concentrate on the most dynamic parts of a sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We refer to this mismatch as the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic uniformity assumption</em>: frames with sharply different local motion difficulty are processed with roughly uniform modeling budget.
This assumption pervades the entire masked pipeline—in training (mask positions ignore local difficulty), in attention (no structural bias toward dynamically compatible frames), and in decoding (uniform sampling regardless of uncertainty).
These are different manifestations of the same mismatch between non-uniform motion dynamics and uniform masked generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we seek a reusable signal that tells the model both <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> motion is dynamically difficult and <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> frames share similar local dynamics.
Such a signal should be motion-grounded, deterministic, and usable across different stages of the masked pipeline without requiring auxiliary models or additional supervision.
Based on this view, we introduce the <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Spectral Descriptor</span> (MSD), a parameter-free frame-level descriptor computed from the short-time spectrum of motion velocity.
For each frame, MSD produces both a scalar complexity summary and a structured spectral profile.
The scalar summary ranks dynamically difficult regions for mask allocation and decoding, while the spectral profile enables pairwise comparison of local motion dynamics for attention computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We then build <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DynMask</span> around this signal with a two-level design.
Its <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">core model</span> uses MSD for <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">content-focused selection</em>, which allocates more reconstruction supervision to dynamically difficult frames, and for <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">motion-aware attention</em>, which biases temporal information flow toward dynamically compatible regions.
Its <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">full model</span> further uses the same signal during iterative decoding through <em id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complexity-aware decoding</em>, where dynamically harder or more uncertain frames are decoded with broader exploration.
Rather than combining several unrelated heuristics, DynMask uses one reusable motion-grounded signal to relax the dynamic uniformity assumption throughout masked motion generation.
Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the core motivation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify the <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic uniformity assumption</em> as a systematic limitation of masked motion generation: frames with sharply different local motion difficulty are treated too uniformly across training, attention, and decoding.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Spectral Descriptor</span> (MSD), a parameter-free signal that provides both scalar complexity ranking and structured spectral comparison, serving as a unified complexity measure throughout the masked pipeline.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DynMask</span>, whose core model uses MSD for content-focused mask selection and motion-aware attention, and whose full model further applies the same signal to complexity-aware decoding, achieving strong results particularly on dynamically complex motion.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Motion Generation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text-to-motion generation has been studied through several major paradigms.
Diffusion-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tevet et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrovich et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shafir et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> generate motion by iterative denoising under text conditioning.
Autoregressive methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> discretize motion into tokens and generate them sequentially.
More recently, masked motion generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinyoanuntapong et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinyoanuntapong et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> has emerged as a strong alternative by combining discrete tokenization with iterative parallel refinement, offering a favorable trade-off among generation quality, decoding efficiency, and editing flexibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond text conditioning, motion generation has also been studied under richer control settings.
Inter-person conditioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruiz-Ponce et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruiz-Ponce et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, key-pose and trajectory guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karunratanakul et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrovich et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, fine-grained editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, human–object interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and scene-aware motion generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> all study how to impose stronger structural or physical constraints on motion synthesis.
Related control settings also appear in audio-driven co-speech gesture generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025e</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and music-conditioned dance generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>.
These works differ in conditioning signals and generation objectives, but many share common backbones such as diffusion models or VQ-VAE-based discrete tokenization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work builds on the masked text-to-motion paradigm.
Rather than introducing a new tokenizer or backbone, we focus on a limitation shared by current masked motion generators: they still treat motion frames too uniformly even though local dynamic complexity varies substantially over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Masked Motion Modeling and Masking Strategies</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Masked motion generation builds on masked prediction in language and vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
A standard pipeline first tokenizes motion with VQ-VAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, then reconstructs masked tokens conditioned on text and visible context, and finally performs iterative refinement at inference time.
Representative motion models include MoMask <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, MMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinyoanuntapong et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, BAMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pinyoanuntapong et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and KMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More broadly, masking strategy strongly affects what masked models learn.
Existing designs include random masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, loss-based masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bandara et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, and attention-based masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kakogeorgiou et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
While these strategies show the value of non-uniform masking, they are usually semantic, task-dependent, or model-dependent.
For motion generation, a key difficulty comes from <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic complexity</em>: frames can differ sharply in reconstruction difficulty because their local temporal dynamics are very different.
Yet current masked motion generators still remain largely complexity-agnostic, with generic mask schedules, content-only attention, and nearly uniform decoding policies.
Recent works such as HGM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., [n. d.]</a>)</cite> and EchoMask <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025e</a>)</cite> begin to question uniform masking.
Our work differs by introducing a motion-grounded complexity signal computed directly from the short-time spectrum of motion velocity, and by using this signal not only for mask allocation but also for motion-aware attention and, in the full model, decoding-time modulation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build DynMask around one motion-grounded signal, the Motion Spectral Descriptor (MSD), and use it to relax the dynamic uniformity assumption in masked motion generation.
The <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">core model</span> uses MSD in two places: complexity-aware mask selection and motion-aware attention.
The <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">full model</span> further uses the same signal during iterative decoding.
Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Method ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> gives an overview.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29655v1/figures/f2_final.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:410/230;" width="410" height="230" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of DynMask.<span id="S3.F2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
</span>(a)<span id="S3.F2.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Core and full framework.
Given motion tokens from a VQ-VAE and a text condition from a frozen CLIP encoder, DynMask computes one motion-grounded complexity signal, the Motion Spectral Descriptor (MSD), and reuses it throughout masked generation.
In the </span>core model<span id="S3.F2.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, MSD guides content-focused mask selection and motion-aware attention inside the masked transformer.
In the </span>full model<span id="S3.F2.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, the same signal is further used by complexity-aware decoding at inference time.
</span>(b)<span id="S3.F2.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> MSD computation.
For each frame, we compute token-embedding velocity, apply a sliding-window Type-II DCT, and obtain both a frame-level spectral descriptor <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and a scalar complexity summary <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span>(c)<span id="S3.F2.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Component details.
Motion-aware attention blends learned attention logits with MSD-derived spectral similarity using a layer-decayed coefficient, while complexity-aware decoding assigns higher temperature and noise to dynamically harder frames.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Preliminaries: Masked Motion Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly review the masked motion generation pipeline that serves as the basis of our method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion tokenization.</span>
Given a motion sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times D_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times D_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> frames and motion feature dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, a VQ-VAE maps <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to a discrete token sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each token <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}\in\{1,\ldots,V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}\in\{1,\ldots,V\}</annotation></semantics></math> indexes a codebook entry in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{V}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{V}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> produces continuous latents <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}=\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{M})_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}=\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{M})_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are quantized by nearest-neighbor lookup:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=\operatorname*{argmin}_{v}\lVert\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}-\mathbf{e}_{v}\rVert_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mi>v</mi></munder><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\operatorname*{argmin}_{v}\lVert\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}-\mathbf{e}_{v}\rVert_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructs motion from the quantized embeddings:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{M}}=\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{e}_{z_{1}},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{z_{T}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{M}}=\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{e}_{z_{1}},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{z_{T}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-conditioned masked prediction.</span>
Following prior masked motion generation work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we encode the input text with a frozen CLIP text encoder and denote the resulting text condition as <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a token sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, masked generation selects a subset of positions <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subset\{1,\ldots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subset\{1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math> and replaces them with a special <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[MASK]</span> symbol:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}_{t}=\begin{cases}\texttt{[MASK]},&amp;t\in\mathcal{S},\\
z_{t},&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[MASK]</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{t}=\begin{cases}\texttt{[MASK]},&amp;t\in\mathcal{S},\\
z_{t},&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">A transformer <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the masked sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and text condition <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> as input and predicts the original tokens at masked positions.
Training minimizes the cross-entropy loss over the masked subset:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=-\sum_{t\in\mathcal{S}}\log p_{\theta}(z_{t}\mid\tilde{\mathbf{z}},\mathbf{c})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=-\sum_{t\in\mathcal{S}}\log p_{\theta}(z_{t}\mid\tilde{\mathbf{z}},\mathbf{c}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">As in MaskGIT-style training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, the total masking ratio follows a cosine schedule <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(r)=\cos(\pi r/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(r)=\cos(\pi r/2)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, while masked positions are usually sampled uniformly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Iterative decoding.</span>
At inference time, the token sequence is initialized as fully masked:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{(0)}=(\texttt{[MASK]},\ldots,\texttt{[MASK]})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[MASK]</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[MASK]</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{(0)}=(\texttt{[MASK]},\ldots,\texttt{[MASK]}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The model predicts masked tokens in parallel, keeps high-confidence predictions, and remasks uncertain positions over multiple refinement steps until the full sequence is decoded.
This pipeline is effective, but it still treats frames with very different local motion difficulty too uniformly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Motivation: Dynamic Complexity is Highly Non-Uniform</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The masked pipeline above is largely complexity-agnostic.
In practice, however, motion difficulty changes strongly over time: static poses and smooth transitions are easy to infer from context, whereas kicks, spins, jumps, and abrupt transitions are much harder.
Our empirical analysis (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Complexity-Stratified Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>) confirms that masked generators degrade much more on dynamic actions, with the largest failures concentrated around frames with strong local dynamics.
We refer to this issue as the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic uniformity assumption</em>, and our goal is to relax it by introducing a motion-grounded complexity signal to guide where and how the pipeline allocates its modeling effort.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Motion Spectral Descriptor</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview.</span>
We measure local dynamic complexity from the short-time spectrum of motion velocity.
The key intuition is simple: static or slowly changing motion has concentrated low-frequency energy, while fast and irregular motion produces richer temporal spectrum.
This makes short-time spectral content a natural descriptor of local dynamic complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature-space velocity.</span>
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathrm{Embed}(z_{t})\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Embed</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathrm{Embed}(z_{t})\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the token embedding at frame <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first compute the temporal first difference:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\qquad t=2,\ldots,T," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t-1},\qquad t=2,\ldots,T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}=\mathbf{v}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}=\mathbf{v}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for boundary handling.
We use velocity rather than position because our target is dynamic complexity, namely how rapidly and irregularly motion changes over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sliding-window DCT.</span>
For each frame <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract a local temporal window of velocity vectors of length <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, centered at <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply a Type-II DCT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmed et al., 1974</a>)</cite> along the temporal axis:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{k,d}=\sum_{n=0}^{W-1}v_{n,d}\cos\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{W}\Bigl(n+\tfrac{1}{2}\Bigr)k\Bigr),\qquad k=0,\ldots,W-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>W</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k,d}=\sum_{n=0}^{W-1}v_{n,d}\cos\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{W}\Bigl(n+\tfrac{1}{2}\Bigr)k\Bigr),\qquad k=0,\ldots,W-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> indexes feature dimensions.
We then aggregate the spectrum across dimensions using the L2 norm:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}=\sqrt{\sum_{d}F_{k,d}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>d</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}=\sqrt{\sum_{d}F_{k,d}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The resulting frequency response is L2-normalized to form the <span id="S3.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Spectral Descriptor</span> (MSD) at frame <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{t}=\frac{(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{W-1})}{\lVert(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{W-1})\rVert_{2}+\epsilon}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{t}=\frac{(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{W-1})}{\lVert(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{W-1})\rVert_{2}+\epsilon}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalar complexity summary.</span>
The full vector <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> captures local spectral shape and is useful for comparing pairs of frames.
For scalar complexity ranking, we use the mean spectral activation:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(t)=\bar{\phi}_{t}=\frac{1}{W}\sum_{k=0}^{W-1}\phi_{t,k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>W</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(t)=\bar{\phi}_{t}=\frac{1}{W}\sum_{k=0}^{W-1}\phi_{t,k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(t)</annotation></semantics></math> only as a compact proxy for local dynamic complexity.
It is not meant to measure semantic importance or full motion difficulty, but specifically the dynamic complexity of local motion evolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why not use velocity alone.</span>
Joint velocity magnitude is a strong scalar proxy for motion difficulty, but it only measures how large the movement is.
In contrast, MSD provides both
(i) a scalar complexity summary <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for ranking difficult regions and
(ii) a structured spectral profile <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for pairwise frame comparison.
This second property is important because our method uses the same signal not only for mask allocation, but also for motion-aware attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Core Model: Complexity-Aware Mask Selection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Algorithms <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.6. Full Model Extension: Complexity-Aware Decoding ‣ 3. Method ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Our first and most direct use of MSD is to guide where reconstruction supervision should be concentrated during training.
The default masked pipeline selects masked positions uniformly at random.
For motion, this underuses dynamically difficult regions, even though these are often the regions where reconstruction is hardest.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Content-Focused Selection.</span>
We score each frame from two complementary views.
The first is dynamic complexity derived from MSD:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}^{\text{dyn}}=\text{z-score}(\bar{\phi}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>dyn</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>z-score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}^{\text{dyn}}=\text{z-score}(\bar{\phi}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3. Motion Spectral Descriptor ‣ 3. Method ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
The second is semantic salience with respect to the text condition:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}^{\text{sem}}=\text{z-score}\left(\frac{\langle\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathbf{c}\rangle}{\lVert\mathbf{x}_{t}\rVert\,\lVert\mathbf{c}\rVert}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>sem</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>z-score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}^{\text{sem}}=\text{z-score}\left(\frac{\langle\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathbf{c}\rangle}{\lVert\mathbf{x}_{t}\rVert\,\lVert\mathbf{c}\rVert}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The dynamic score emphasizes frames with complex local motion, while the semantic score preserves frames that are strongly aligned with the text condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a total masking budget <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we allocate two quotas:
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\lambda)K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\lambda)K</annotation></semantics></math> positions are selected from frames with high dynamic-complexity score, and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda K</annotation></semantics></math> positions are selected from frames with high semantic-salience score.
An optional temporal expansion radius <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> extends each selected position to nearby frames, which improves coverage around important temporal regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A key point is that we change <em id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> positions are masked, but not <em id="S3.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how many</em>.
The total masking ratio still follows the standard cosine schedule.
This isolates the role of complexity-aware supervision allocation from the overall amount of masking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Core Model: Motion-Aware Attention</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our second core use of MSD is to provide a motion-grounded bias for self-attention.
Standard attention measures similarity in a learned feature space.
For motion, however, two frames may play similar dynamic roles even if their raw embeddings are not close.
Examples include repeated gait phases, similar airborne phases, or temporally distant segments with comparable rhythmic structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spectral similarity.</span>
Given MSD vectors <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for frames <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we define their spectral similarity as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S^{\text{freq}}_{i,j}=-\frac{1}{\tau}\sum_{k=0}^{W-1}w_{k}\bigl(\phi_{i,k}-\phi_{j,k}\bigr)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>freq</mtext></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\text{freq}}_{i,j}=-\frac{1}{\tau}\sum_{k=0}^{W-1}w_{k}\bigl(\phi_{i,k}-\phi_{j,k}\bigr)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a scale factor and</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}=\frac{\exp(-k/3)}{\sum_{k^{\prime}}\exp(-k^{\prime}/3)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=\frac{\exp(-k/3)}{\sum_{k^{\prime}}\exp(-k^{\prime}/3)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">assigns larger weight to lower-frequency bands, which tend to be more stable indicators of local motion structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attention fusion.</span>
Let <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard self-attention logits.
We blend the learned attention logits with the spectral similarity prior:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}_{i,j}=(1-\alpha_{\ell})\hat{A}_{i,j}+\alpha_{\ell}\hat{S}^{\text{freq}}_{i,j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>freq</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}_{i,j}=(1-\alpha_{\ell})\hat{A}_{i,j}+\alpha_{\ell}\hat{S}^{\text{freq}}_{i,j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}^{\text{freq}}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>freq</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}^{\text{freq}}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are z-score normalized over valid positions, and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is a layer-dependent fusion weight:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{\ell}=\alpha_{0}\exp(-\ell/3)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\ell}=\alpha_{0}\exp(-\ell/3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">This schedule places stronger motion prior in earlier layers, where coarse temporal organization is formed, and gives later layers more freedom to refine motion from learned content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion-aware attention block.</span>
The fused logits <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed through the usual attention computation to obtain the attention map, which is then applied to value features before the feed-forward layer.
In this way, the spectral prior changes <em id="S3.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> information is routed across time rather than adding a separate supervision term.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Full Model Extension: Complexity-Aware Decoding</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two components above define the <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">core model</span> of DynMask.
As illustrated in Algorithms <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.6. Full Model Extension: Complexity-Aware Decoding ‣ 3. Method ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in the <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">full model</span>, we additionally use MSD during iterative decoding.
This decoding-time component is optional.
It is not required for the main idea of complexity-aware supervision and motion-aware attention, but it provides an additional way to make generation sensitive to local motion difficulty at inference time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploration score.</span>
At decoding step <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute a frame-wise exploration score that combines dynamic complexity and model uncertainty:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{t}=\sigma\bigl(\lambda_{D}\hat{k}_{t}+(1-\lambda_{D})\hat{u}_{t}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}=\sigma\bigl(\lambda_{D}\hat{k}_{t}+(1-\lambda_{D})\hat{u}_{t}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function. The normalized complexity term is</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}_{t}=\operatorname{zscore}(\bar{\phi}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>zscore</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}_{t}=\operatorname{zscore}(\bar{\phi}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The normalized uncertainty term is</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{t}=\operatorname{zscore}(1-\max_{v}p_{t,v})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>zscore</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>v</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{t}=\operatorname{zscore}(1-\max_{v}p_{t,v}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token-level temperature modulation.</span>
We use <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to modulate token-level sampling temperature and noise:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}=T^{\mathrm{global}}(1+\beta b_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>global</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}=T^{\mathrm{global}}(1+\beta b_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=\sigma_{\max}b_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=\sigma_{\max}b_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token-level temperature modulation.</span>
We use <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to modulate token-level sampling temperature and noise:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=T^{\text{global}}\cdot(1+\beta b_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mtext>global</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=T^{\text{global}}\cdot(1+\beta b_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma_{\max}\cdot b_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma_{\max}\cdot b_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\text{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mtext>global</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\text{global}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a step-dependent global temperature, <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of adaptation, and <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum noise scale.
Frames that are more complex or more uncertain are allowed broader exploration, while simpler and more confident frames are decoded more conservatively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> DynMask training (core model)</figcaption>
