<td id="S4.T4.6.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DDEBFF;"><span id="S4.T4.6.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#DDEBFF;">2.879<math id="S4.T4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DDEBFF;" mathbackground="#DDEBFF" mathsize="0.780em">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T4.6.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:78%;">.006</span></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Additional ablations in the appendix examine further implementation choices, including the input signal used to compute MSD, the fusion rule for motion-aware attention, the layer schedule for the spectral prior, simpler alternatives such as direct loss reweighting and feature concatenation, and the design of dual-quota mask allocation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Complexity-Stratified Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central claim of this work is that complexity-aware generation should help most where motion is dynamically difficult.
We verify this claim through three complementary analyses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-category breakdown.</span>
We first divide HumanML3D test motions into static and dynamic groups by action semantics.
Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.4. Complexity-Stratified Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Not All Frames Are Equal: Complexity-Aware Masked Motion Generation via Motion Spectral Descriptors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that the masked baseline degrades much more on the dynamic group (FID 0.227) than on the static group (FID 0.077), a 2.96<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> gap.
DynMask reduces this gap to 1.77<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that complexity-aware generation is most helpful.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>. </span><span id="S4.T5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Per-category FID<span id="S4.T5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on HumanML3D.
Dynamic actions are substantially harder for the masked baseline, while DynMask reduces the gap.
Motions are classified by keyword matching into static (3030 samples) and dynamic (1168 samples) groups.</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T5.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
